    },
    {
      "design": "d3r0B",
      "good": "Intact keys make lookup much easier, while most translations remain readable in short lines. The table is substantially more compact than A without requiring every message to stay on one line. Headers are clear and rows remain easy to track across the full render.",
      "problems": "The longest message produces a disproportionately tall row, with awkward Japanese and Chinese breaks and one measured break inside a word. On first sight the viewport shows the keys and English, but only a sliver of Chinese; comparing translations requires scrolling, and the key will leave view as the reader moves right.",
      "cause": "The 110px Chinese, 155px Japanese and 120px Arabic columns constrain the longer messages. The 227px key column preserves identifiers but consumes most of the initial viewport; the full table is 733px wide."
    },
    {
      "design": "d3r0C",
      "good": "Every entry fits on one line, giving the full table a calm, regular rhythm and making individual strings easy to read. Keys and translations can be compared cleanly when the whole table is visible.",
      "problems": "The large horizontal span makes comparison on a phone laborious. The initial viewport cuts through English and shows none of the other languages, so even the reference text requires scrolling. Extra width is devoted to keeping short phrases on one line where natural wrapping would be acceptable.",
      "cause": "The 217px English, 205px Chinese, 304px Japanese and 208px Arabic columns accommodate their widest entries, producing a 1162px table."
    }
